1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Let's get into building your first agent in code.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
When you want to run an agent yourself, you hit a problem.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Where do you even start?

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The Cloud Agent SDK answers that with Query,

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
one clean entry point that runs the very same loop powering Cloud Code.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Let's dig into it.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's define the pieces.

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The Agent SDK is a library.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
It packages the same tools, agent loop, and context management that run inside Cloud Code,

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
and it ships for both Python and TypeScript.

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Query is the primary entry point into that library.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
You call it, and it runs the agent loop for you.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Here's the part people miss.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
The SDK is standalone.

15
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
You don't need the Cloud Code command line tool installed anywhere.

16
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
You import the package, call Query, and you are running an agent.

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
And by default, every call to Query starts a fresh session,

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
with no memory of any earlier call.

19
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
That fresh start is the default you build around.

20
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,000
So how do you get running?

21
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
In Python, you install with pip install Cloud Agent SDK,

22
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
and you need Python 3.10 or newer.

23
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
In TypeScript, you pull the at anthropic AI Cloud Agent SDK package from npm.

24
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Authentication is just an environment variable.

25
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Set anthropic API key, and you are ready.

26
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
If your company runs Cloud through a cloud provider,

27
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
you flip an environment flag instead.

28
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
There are flags for Amazon Bedrock and for Google's Vertex platform.

29
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
So you route through the provider your infrastructure already uses.

30
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Notice what you didn't do.

31
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
You never installed a separate command line tool.

32
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
One import, one credential, and the whole agent loop is available to your code.

33
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
You will hear that the SDK has two entry points,

34
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Query and Cloud SDK Client,

35
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and that they are somehow rival designs.

36
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Let me correct that now, because it trips people up.

37
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
They aren't two separate loops.

38
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
They are two Python ways into the exact same agent loop.

39
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Query is the function you reach for most.

40
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
It runs a task and starts a fresh session every call.

41
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Cloud SDK Client is an object that holds one session open across several calls

42
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and lets you interrupt a run in progress.

43
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
The real difference is session lifecycle and interrupts, not the underlying engine.

44
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Reach for Query first,

45
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
and move to the client when you need a persistent session or live interruption.

46
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
So how do you actually control the agent?

47
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
You pass options into Query.

48
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
The system prompt sets the agent's role

49
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
and the standing instructions it follows on every turn.

50
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
The tools list decides which actions the model is even allowed to take,

51
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
from reading files to calling your own functions.

52
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The model option picks which Cloud model runs the loop,

53
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and max turns caps how many tool using turns the loop will take before it stops.

54
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
That last cap matters more than it looks.

55
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Without a bound, an agent can loop far longer than you intended.

56
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The same query, function, configured differently, gives you very different agents.

57
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
When you call Query, what comes back isn't a finished string.

58
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
It is an async iterator.

59
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
You loop over it, reading each message as the agent produces it,

60
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
from its reasoning, to its tool calls, to its final answer.

61
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
That streaming shape is why the SDK feels live.

62
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
You watch the run unfold instead of blocking on one big response.

63
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
The second thing to hold on to is the session.

64
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Each call opens a fresh session, with no memory of any prior call.

65
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
If you want the agent to remember an earlier conversation,

66
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
that persistence is something you opt into, not something you get for free.

67
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Design for that fresh start, and the whole run happens inside your own process,

68
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
with no command line tool running behind it.

69
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Let's look at what actually flows through that stream.

70
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
The messages are typed, and they arrive in the order the agent produces them.

71
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
First comes an init message.

72
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
It opens the run, and reports the resolved configuration,

73
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
the model, the tools, the system prompt.

74
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Then you see assistant messages carrying the model's reasoning and any tool calls,

75
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
followed by the results those tools return.

76
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
This back and forth repeats as the agent works.

77
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Finally, a result message closes the run.

78
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It carries the total cost, the token usage,

79
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
and a status telling you whether the run succeeded or hit a limit.

80
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Iterating that sequence is how your code follows the agent live, message by message.

81
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Let me pull the run's character into three traits,

82
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
because they shape everything you build next.

83
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
First, it's standalone.

84
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
The run needs no command line tool.

85
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
The library alone carries the full agent loop into your process.

86
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Second, it's streamed.

87
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Messages arrive as an async iterator, and you read them live as the agent works,

88
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
rather than waiting for one final block.

89
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Third, it's session-scoped.

90
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Each call is its own fresh session,

91
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and any memory across calls is something you opt into deliberately.

92
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Hold those three together and query.

93
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Stops feeling like magic.

94
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
It's a standalone, streaming, session-scoped call into one well-defined agent loop.

95
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Let's tie it together into a path you can actually follow.

96
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Your first call can be tiny.

97
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Pass a prompt, take the default options, and loop over the messages

98
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
just to watch the agent loop run end-to-end.

99
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Once that works, add tools so the agent can take real actions

100
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
instead of only answering in text.

101
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Then, before you hand it anything long or expensive,

102
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
add a maxTurnsBound so a stuck run can't spin forever.

103
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Notice that you never switched entry points to do any of this.

104
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
The same query, call, that ran your two-line example

105
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
is the one that runs a tool-using agent.

106
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
It grows with you as the task grows.

107
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Here's what to take away.

108
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Query runs the agent SDK's agent loop right inside your code.

109
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
A single function, configured with a prompt, tools, and a turncap,

110
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
takes you from a first call to a real agent.

111
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Just remember, query and the client share a single loop, not two.

112
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
That covers writing your first agent with query.

113
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

